[0;33mThe figure has four magnitude–frequency distribution (MFD) panels, each showing:

- **Black line:** cumulative FMD
- **Gray dots:** incremental FMD
- **Orange solid vertical line:** **Mc** from max curvature
- **Green dashed vertical line:** **fixed Mc = 1.5**
- **Green dashed sloped line:** GR fit using fixed Mc
- **Orange dashed sloped line:** GR fit using auto Mc

## 1) Interevent full region
- The **cumulative FMD** is smooth and high-count, extending to about **M 5**.
- The **incremental FMD** peaks around **M ~1.0–1.3**, then declines.
- The **auto Mc** marker is at about **M ~1.25**.
- The **fixed Mc=1.5** marker lies slightly to the right, at **M 1.5**.
- The **two GR fits** are very similar over most of the linear range; they nearly overlap from roughly **M 1.5 to 4+**.
- At the largest magnitudes, both fits remain close, with only small divergence.

## 2) Background region
- This panel has the **highest counts at low magnitudes** among the subsets shown, with cumulative counts above **10^3**.
- The **incremental FMD** peaks lower, around **M ~0.7–1.0**, and is broader than in the local cores.
- The **auto Mc** is lower than in the other panels, at about **M ~0.8–0.9**.
- The **fixed Mc=1.5** marker is much farther right, creating a larger gap between the two Mc choices.
- The **orange auto-Mc GR fit** starts earlier and is visibly **shallower / less steep** than the green fixed-Mc fit.
- The **green fixed-Mc GR fit** follows the higher-magnitude tail more closely and drops faster.
- This panel shows the **clearest difference between fitting with auto Mc and fixed Mc**.

## 3) Mw 6.4 core 5 km full
- The cumulative FMD is similar in shape to the interevent full region but with fewer events.
- The incremental FMD peaks near **M ~1.0–1.2**.
- The **auto Mc** is around **M ~1.3–1.35**.
- The **fixed Mc=1.5** marker is only slightly higher.
- The **orange and green GR fits** are again very close, with modest separation at larger magnitudes.
- The cumulative curve appears to bend down somewhat above about **M 3.5–4**, likely reflecting lower counts in the tail.

## 4) Mw 7.1 core 5 km full
- This subset has the **fewest events** overall, with cumulative counts much lower than the other panels.
- The incremental FMD is sparse, peaking around **M ~0.9–1.2**.
- The **auto Mc** is near **M ~1.25–1.3**.
- The **fixed Mc=1.5** marker is again slightly to the right.
- The **GR fits** are very similar and overlap across most of the usable magnitude range.
- Because of the smaller sample, the cumulative tail is noisier and shows stronger stepwise drops above about **M 3–4**.

## Visible differences among the regions/subsets
- **Background region:** lowest auto Mc, strongest mismatch between **auto Mc** and **fixed Mc=1.5** fits, and the most noticeable difference in GR slope depending on Mc choice.
- **Interevent full region:** largest overall magnitude span to **~M 5**, good linear GR behavior above Mc, and little sensitivity to using auto vs fixed Mc.
- **Mw 6.4 core:** similar to interevent full but with fewer events; Mc estimates remain close to 1.5, and both GR fits are still consistent.
- **Mw 7.1 core:** sparsest dataset; same general behavior but with more scatter and less stable high-magnitude tail.

## Overall interpretation from the panels
- In **three panels** (interevent full, Mw 6.4 core, Mw 7.1 core), **auto Mc is close to 1.5**, so the fixed and auto GR fits are nearly the same.
- The **background region** stands out because **auto Mc is substantially lower than 1.5**, causing a noticeably different Gutenberg–Richter fit when the lower threshold is used.
- The local **core subsets** have fewer events and noisier tails, but their MFDs still show approximately linear Gutenberg–Richter behavior above **Mc ~1.3–1.5**.[0m

